Z||000007||WampusCat||06-15-2001||08:24 PM||||Many pilots are seriously injured or killed by their ejection seats.  These are people who are trained and repeatedly briefed on proper technique and body position to avoid injury.  In a car wreck, you're very likely to have your head turned or be out of alignment and get a broken neck or back.  I'll take my chances in the wreck with seatbelts on thanks...oh, wait...this is just a joke right...oh...hahaha!||||reg||1
Z||000008||SteveJ||06-15-2001||08:36 PM||||The "take a chance" mindset is what guides those who don't wear belts so I don't think ejection seat injuries would deter them from getting one for their car.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||11:27 PM||||well now this is gonna get real interesting, since they will refuse to be strapped into the ejection seat too. I wonder at what altitude they will fall out of the seat - cause the parachute is usually attached to the seat, not to the pilot, right?<P>   and even if not, they wont want to be strapped to the parachute either.<P>  and I guess you will need an 'under bridge' detector, in a tunnel detector, under tree limbs, powerlines, road signs...<P>  Screw it! I have a better idea. If they wont wear their seat belts, the ejection seat will only kill them anyway:<P>   so lets replace the air bags with auto-deploying body bags. As soon as they hit somthing BANG! they are bagged and ready for the meat wagon.<P>   will save the rescue crew alot of trauma.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Steve Normington||06-16-2001||01:17 AM||||What about rearward ejection? When a crash is detected, the roof blows off, the seat tilts all the way back, and the person is ejected with just enough force to zero out the cars forward speed and they land at zero altitude, zero velocity. This way you don't have to worry about bridges, tunnels, power lines, etc.<P>Of course, If they are being tailgated...<P>------------------<BR>This is a copy of 84Bill's copy of RiceCooker's witty signature,<BR>It makes me a lemming^2||||reg||1
